[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Docker Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding Containerization and the Docker Model",
        "content": "Containerization is an approach to packaging an application together with the libraries, runtime dependencies, configuration, and supporting files it needs to execute. Docker provides tools for building, distributing, and running these packaged environments as containers. Instead of installing every application dependency directly onto a server, a team can define the environment in an image and start containers from that image whenever required. Containers share the host operating-system kernel while maintaining isolated process, filesystem, and network views. This generally makes them lighter to start and operate than complete virtual machines, although containers still consume CPU, memory, storage, and network resources. Docker does not automatically make an application secure or portable in every situation. Compatibility still depends on the operating system, architecture, kernel features, external services, and runtime configuration. In a DevOps workflow, containerization helps developers and operations teams use a consistent application package across development, testing, and deployment environments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Containers Compared with Virtual Machines",
        "content": "A virtual machine runs a guest operating system on virtualized hardware managed by a hypervisor. Each virtual machine normally includes its own operating-system kernel and system services, which provides a strong separation boundary but adds memory, storage, and startup overhead. A container instead isolates application processes while sharing the host kernel. The container image contains the application filesystem and required user-space components, but it does not normally contain a separate bootable kernel. This allows containers to start quickly and makes it practical to run many application instances on a host. The trade-off is that containers depend on compatible host-kernel capabilities and require careful configuration of isolation and privileges. Virtual machines remain useful when workloads need different kernels or stronger infrastructure separation. Containers are especially useful for packaging microservices, build agents, APIs, and repeatable test environments. In production, organizations may run containers inside virtual machines to combine infrastructure isolation with application-level packaging."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Docker Images, Containers, and the Relationship Between Them",
        "content": "A Docker image is a read-only template used to create containers. It contains a filesystem assembled from image layers and metadata such as the default command, environment settings, and working directory. A container is a runtime instance created from an image. When Docker creates a container, it adds a writable layer for runtime changes while retaining the underlying image layers as shared read-only data. Multiple containers can be created from the same image without rebuilding the application package for each instance. Changes made inside one container do not normally modify the original image or another container. If a container is removed, data stored only in its writable layer is also removed. Important application data should therefore be placed in persistent volumes or external storage. Understanding this distinction prevents common operational mistakes, such as expecting a changed container to update an image or assuming that files written inside a temporary container will survive its removal."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Container Lifecycle and Essential Docker Operations",
        "content": "A container follows a lifecycle that begins when it is created from an image and proceeds through runtime states such as created, running, paused, stopped, and removed. The docker create command prepares a container without starting its main process, while docker start starts an existing container. The docker run command combines creation and startup in one operation. Administrators use docker ps to inspect running containers and docker ps -a to include stopped containers. The docker stop command requests a graceful shutdown by sending a termination signal and allowing a configured timeout before forced termination. The docker kill command can terminate a container more abruptly. The docker restart command stops and starts a container again, while docker rm removes a stopped container. A container usually exits when its configured primary process finishes or fails. Production operators should inspect exit codes, logs, restart policies, health checks, and resource conditions instead of repeatedly restarting an unhealthy application without identifying the cause."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Dockerfiles, Build Contexts, and Image Creation",
        "content": "A Dockerfile is a text-based set of instructions that describes how to construct an application image. Common instructions include FROM for the base image, WORKDIR for the working directory, COPY for adding files, RUN for build-time commands, ENV for environment variables, EXPOSE for documenting an intended listening port, and CMD or ENTRYPOINT for defining container startup behavior. Docker sends a build context to the builder, which contains the files available to instructions such as COPY and ADD. An unnecessarily large context increases transfer time and can accidentally include credentials, local dependencies, or generated files. A .dockerignore file excludes unwanted paths from that context. The docker build command creates an image from the Dockerfile and context, while modern BuildKit-based builds provide improved caching and build capabilities. A reliable Dockerfile should use reproducible dependency installation, minimize unnecessary layers and packages, avoid embedding secrets, and produce a predictable runtime command. Teams should test the built image rather than assuming that a successful image build proves the application works correctly."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Image Layers, Build Cache, and Efficient Builds",
        "content": "Docker images are assembled from filesystem layers that represent changes introduced during image construction. Layers can be reused by multiple images and containers, reducing repeated storage and build work. Docker's build cache can reuse a previous instruction result when the instruction and its relevant inputs have not changed. The order of Dockerfile instructions therefore affects build efficiency. For example, copying dependency manifests and installing dependencies before copying frequently changing application source can allow the dependency layer to remain cached during ordinary source-code changes. If an instruction or its inputs change, later dependent layers may need to be rebuilt. A large RUN instruction that downloads packages and leaves temporary files behind can increase image size even when those files are no longer needed. Multi-stage builds can keep compilers and build tools out of the final runtime image. Efficient builds should balance cache reuse, clarity, reproducibility, and security rather than optimizing only for the smallest possible image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Image Registries, Tags, and Immutable Digests",
        "content": "A container registry stores and distributes container images so that build systems, developers, and deployment platforms can retrieve the same application package. Docker Hub is a public registry, while organizations may operate private registries or use managed cloud registry services. An image reference commonly includes a registry hostname, namespace, repository name, and tag. Tags such as development, staging, or version numbers make images easier to identify, but a tag can be reassigned to point to different image content. A digest identifies image content using a cryptographic content identifier and is therefore more suitable for recording the exact artifact that was tested and approved. A release pipeline should build an image, publish it to an authorized registry, capture its digest, scan that exact artifact, and deploy the same digest. Registry credentials should be supplied through protected authentication mechanisms, and access should follow least privilege. Avoid embedding credentials in image layers or source-controlled configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Container Networking and Persistent Storage Basics",
        "content": "Containers often need network communication with other containers, host services, and external systems. Docker provides network drivers such as bridge, host, none, and overlay, each supporting different connectivity and isolation requirements. A user-defined bridge network is commonly useful for connecting related containers on one Docker host, because containers on that network can communicate using Docker-provided name resolution. Publishing a container port with a host-to-container port mapping makes a service reachable through the host network interface, subject to firewall and routing rules. Container storage also requires deliberate planning. The writable container layer is temporary and should not be treated as durable application storage. Volumes provide persistent storage managed by Docker, while bind mounts connect a host path to a container path. Database files, uploaded documents, and other important state should use an appropriate persistent storage design with backups and access controls. Networking and storage choices should be documented because incorrect port exposure or mount permissions can cause both outages and security risks."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Running Containers Reliably in Development and Production",
        "content": "A container that starts successfully is not necessarily ready to serve production traffic. Reliable operation requires suitable health checks, resource limits, logging, restart behavior, configuration management, and monitoring. Applications should normally write operational logs to standard output and standard error so that the container platform can collect them. Environment variables or mounted configuration files can supply environment-specific settings, but sensitive values should be provided through a secret-management mechanism rather than baked into the image. CPU and memory limits help prevent one container from consuming all host resources, although limits must be selected using workload measurements. Health checks can report whether an application is functioning, but they should test meaningful application behavior and avoid excessive dependencies. Restart policies can recover from certain process failures, but they cannot replace root-cause analysis or orchestration. Before release, teams should scan images, use trusted base images, run with minimal privileges, test graceful shutdown, and verify that persistent data and external dependencies behave as expected."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Docker Architecture",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Docker Client-Server Architecture and Request Flow",
        "content": "Docker commonly follows a client-server architecture. The Docker client is the command-line interface or another application that sends requests to the Docker daemon through a supported API. When an operator runs a command such as docker run, the client sends the requested operation to the daemon rather than directly creating operating-system processes itself. The daemon evaluates the request, checks the required image and configuration, and coordinates container creation through the runtime components. The Docker client and daemon may run on the same host or communicate across a secured remote connection. This separation allows automation systems and user interfaces to control Docker through a consistent interface. It also means that a command can fail because of daemon availability, API compatibility, authorization, or connectivity even when the client executable is installed correctly. Operators should distinguish client-side errors from daemon-side failures by checking the Docker context, daemon status, API response, and relevant service logs."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Docker Engine Components and Their Responsibilities",
        "content": "Docker Engine is the platform that provides the APIs and services used to build, manage, and run containers. Its architecture includes the Docker client, Docker daemon, image and container management capabilities, and integrations with lower-level container runtime components. The daemon, commonly called dockerd, manages Docker objects such as images, containers, networks, and volumes. It receives API requests and coordinates the operations required to satisfy them. The client provides the interface through which users and automation systems issue commands. Container execution is delegated through runtime interfaces to components responsible for preparing and starting processes. Image-building capabilities use a builder such as BuildKit in modern workflows. These components have separate responsibilities, so a problem in image construction is not necessarily a runtime failure, and a daemon API problem is not necessarily an application defect. Understanding these boundaries helps administrators identify the correct service, log source, permission, or configuration setting during troubleshooting."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "The Role of containerd and runc in Container Execution",
        "content": "Docker Engine commonly uses containerd as a container runtime management component and runc as a low-level runtime implementation. When Docker receives a request to start a container, dockerd coordinates the operation with containerd. Containerd manages container lifecycle operations and related resources, including image transfer and snapshot integration. For an actual container process, containerd can invoke a runtime such as runc. Runc implements the Open Container Initiative runtime specification and uses operating-system facilities to configure the process environment, namespaces, control groups, mounts, and other runtime settings. These layers separate high-level Docker object management from lower-level process creation. Not every Docker installation has an identical internal deployment arrangement, and additional runtime integrations may be used. When a container fails to start, operators should identify which layer returned the error instead of assuming every failure originates in dockerd. Service status, runtime logs, storage state, and host-kernel support provide useful evidence."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Open Container Initiative Standards and Runtime Compatibility",
        "content": "The Open Container Initiative, commonly called OCI, defines standards that support interoperability across container technologies. Important specifications include the image specification, runtime specification, and distribution specification. The image specification describes how an image is represented through manifests, configuration metadata, and filesystem layers. The runtime specification describes how a container should be configured and executed on a host. The distribution specification addresses how container content can be transferred and stored through registries. These standards help different tools exchange container images and runtime configurations without requiring every component to use one vendor-specific implementation. Docker images and many other container images follow OCI-compatible formats, while runtimes such as runc implement the OCI runtime specification. Compatibility still depends on supported features, architecture, operating-system requirements, and implementation versions. In a DevOps environment, OCI standards make it easier to use different builders, registries, runtimes, and orchestration platforms within a consistent software delivery process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Linux Namespaces and Container Isolation",
        "content": "Linux namespaces provide isolation by presenting processes with separate views of selected operating-system resources. Depending on the namespaces configured for a container, its processes can see an isolated process identifier space, network interfaces, mount points, hostname, inter-process communication resources, and user identifiers. This helps one container operate without directly seeing all resources available to other containers on the same host. Namespaces do not create a separate kernel; container processes still rely on the host kernel. Isolation therefore depends on correct kernel behavior, runtime configuration, permissions, and security controls. A privileged container or excessive host mounts can weaken the intended separation. User namespaces can help map container identities to different host identities, reducing the impact of some permission mistakes. Administrators should understand which namespaces are enabled and avoid granting unnecessary capabilities or host access. Namespace isolation is an important part of container security, but it must be combined with access control, seccomp, mandatory access controls where available, and safe image practices."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Control Groups and Container Resource Management",
        "content": "Linux control groups, commonly called cgroups, provide mechanisms for accounting for and controlling resource usage by groups of processes. Container runtimes use cgroups to apply resource policies such as CPU allocation, memory limits, and process-related controls. Without suitable resource controls, a container with excessive memory consumption can contribute to host memory pressure and potentially affect unrelated workloads. A memory limit can cause the container's processes to be terminated when the configured limit is exceeded, depending on the host and cgroup behavior. CPU limits influence how much processing time a workload can receive, while CPU shares or weights influence relative scheduling under contention. Docker exposes resource configuration options when creating or updating containers. Administrators should select limits based on observed workload behavior and application requirements rather than choosing arbitrary values. During an incident, inspect container metrics, host memory pressure, cgroup configuration, and termination information to distinguish an application defect from resource exhaustion."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Image Manifests, Content Digests, and Layer Storage",
        "content": "A container image is represented through metadata and filesystem content that can be distributed and reconstructed by an image-capable runtime. An image manifest identifies the configuration and layers associated with an image, while a configuration object records information such as the default command, environment, and runtime settings. Content digests provide identifiers derived from the content itself, allowing clients and registries to verify that retrieved objects match the expected data. Image layers represent filesystem changes and can be reused across images when the content is identical. The local image store maintains the content and metadata needed to create containers. The precise storage implementation depends on the Docker version, operating system, and configured storage backend. Administrators should avoid manually editing internal image-store files because doing so can corrupt metadata or leave the daemon in an inconsistent state. Use Docker-supported commands to inspect, pull, build, tag, and remove images, and use immutable digests when recording release artifacts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "BuildKit and the Modern Docker Image Build Process",
        "content": "BuildKit is a modern build engine used by Docker to execute image build instructions with improved performance and build features. It processes the build definition, evaluates dependencies between build steps, and uses caching where the relevant inputs allow safe reuse. BuildKit supports capabilities such as parallel execution of independent build stages, efficient transfer of build context, cache import and export, and controlled access to build-time secrets. A Dockerfile can use multiple stages to separate compilation from the final runtime image. Build-time secrets should be mounted through supported secret mechanisms rather than passed as ordinary build arguments that may be recorded in build metadata or layers. Build cache is useful for speed, but teams must understand cache invalidation and ensure that security-sensitive updates are not hidden by an inappropriate cache strategy. Build logs and builder configuration help distinguish a build-engine problem from a Docker daemon or runtime problem. Reproducible builds should also pin dependencies and record the inputs used to create the final image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "Docker Networking Architecture and Network Drivers",
        "content": "Docker networking connects containers to one another, the host, and external networks through network objects and drivers. On Linux, bridge networking commonly uses virtual Ethernet interfaces, a software bridge, routing, and network address translation where required. Docker can configure container network endpoints and provide name resolution on user-defined networks. Host networking uses the host network namespace rather than a separate container network namespace, which changes isolation and port behavior. The none driver provides a container with no ordinary external network connectivity through Docker networking. Overlay networking can connect workloads across multiple hosts when the required networking infrastructure is available. Port publishing creates a mapping from a host port to a container port; it does not mean the application is automatically secure or reachable from every network. Firewalls, routing, DNS, address allocation, and host configuration all affect connectivity. Operators should inspect network membership, endpoint configuration, published ports, and host firewall rules when diagnosing communication failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-8-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "Storage Drivers, Volumes, and Filesystem Mounts",
        "content": "Docker separates image filesystem layers from the writable filesystem changes made by a running container. A storage driver or snapshotter manages how image layers and container writable filesystems are represented on the host. The exact implementation varies by platform and Docker configuration, so administrators should use supported inspection commands rather than assuming a particular storage driver. Volumes provide persistent storage managed independently from a container's writable layer. Bind mounts expose a specified host path inside the container and are useful for development or controlled access to host files, but they require careful permission and path management. Mounting a host directory over an image directory can hide files that were originally present in the image. Database and application state should be stored using an appropriate persistence design with backup, recovery, and access-control procedures. When investigating storage problems, inspect available disk capacity, inode usage, mount options, ownership, volume metadata, and daemon logs before deleting data or changing internal storage directories."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Docker Installation",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Docker Engine Components and Installation Choices",
        "content": "Docker installation begins with understanding which Docker components are required on the target machine. Docker Engine is the core runtime responsible for creating and managing containers. It includes the Docker daemon, the Docker CLI, and the REST API used by clients to communicate with the daemon. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes, while the CLI provides commands such as docker pull, docker build, and docker run. On Linux, Engine normally runs as a system service. On Windows and macOS, Docker Desktop provides an integrated environment that can run Linux containers through a lightweight virtualized Linux environment. Installation choices depend on the operating system, security requirements, administrative permissions, and whether the machine is a developer workstation or a server. Production Linux hosts often install Docker Engine from a trusted vendor repository, while developers may prefer Docker Desktop for its integrated interface and Kubernetes options. Before installation, confirm CPU architecture, supported operating-system version, available disk space, network access, and organizational policy. Select a supported installation method and avoid mixing packages from unrelated repositories because conflicting versions can cause daemon startup failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Installing Docker Engine on Ubuntu Linux",
        "content": "On Ubuntu, a controlled Docker Engine installation should use the official Docker package repository rather than an unknown installation script. First identify the Ubuntu release and architecture, then update the package index and install the prerequisites required to download repository metadata securely. Add Docker official repository information and its signing key using the currently documented keyring method. Refresh the package index after adding the repository so the package manager can discover the Engine packages. Install Docker Engine, the CLI, containerd, Buildx, and the Compose plugin when they are required by the project. After installation, check the daemon with systemctl status docker and start or enable the service when necessary. Run docker version and docker info to verify that both the client and server respond. A successful package installation does not automatically prove that containers can start, so run a small test container and inspect its exit status. In managed environments, pin or control package versions through configuration management so an unattended upgrade does not unexpectedly change runtime behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "Installing Docker Desktop on Windows",
        "content": "Docker Desktop on Windows provides a convenient environment for building and running Linux containers from a Windows workstation. Before installing it, check the supported Windows edition, hardware virtualization support, available memory, and the requirements for the WSL 2 backend. Enable the required Windows features and install or update WSL according to the current Docker Desktop documentation. Install Docker Desktop using the approved installer, start the application, and select the intended container backend. The WSL 2 backend allows Docker to run a Linux-based container environment while developers continue using Windows terminals and editors. After startup, verify that the Docker CLI is available in PowerShell and that docker version displays both client and server information. Test a container and confirm that its output is returned. If the daemon is unavailable, investigate whether Docker Desktop has finished starting, whether WSL is healthy, and whether another virtualization or security setting is interfering. In corporate environments, follow approved installation and licensing policies rather than bypassing administrator controls."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-3-section-4",
        "title": "Installing Docker on macOS",
        "content": "Docker Desktop for macOS provides Docker CLI and Engine functionality through a managed Linux virtual machine because Linux containers require a Linux kernel. Select the installer that matches the Mac hardware architecture, such as Apple silicon or Intel, and verify that the macOS version is supported. Install Docker Desktop using the approved distribution method, move or configure the application as instructed, and launch it so the Linux environment can initialize. The first startup may require permission changes or additional resource allocation. Once the Docker whale indicator shows that the engine is running, open Terminal and execute docker version. Confirm that the client and server sections are both present. Run a small test container to validate image download, container startup, and output handling. Developers should configure CPU, memory, disk-image size, and file-sharing settings according to workload needs. If containers cannot access project files, check the configured file-sharing locations and host permissions. Keep Docker Desktop updated through an approved release process, especially when projects depend on specific Compose or Buildx features."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-3-section-5",
        "title": "Post-Installation Verification and First Container",
        "content": "Post-installation verification must test more than whether the Docker command exists. Start by checking docker version to confirm that the CLI can communicate with the daemon. Use docker info to inspect server details such as storage driver, operating system, architecture, and available runtime configuration. Run docker run hello-world or another approved lightweight test image. This test exercises image lookup, registry communication, image download, container creation, process execution, and output delivery. Inspect docker ps -a to confirm the container lifecycle and docker images to see the downloaded image. If the test fails, classify the failure before changing settings: a missing command indicates a CLI or PATH problem, a daemon connection error indicates service or socket access, and a pull failure may indicate DNS, proxy, TLS, or registry connectivity. Capture the exact error and relevant command output. For repeatable workstation setup, automate these checks and record the installed Engine and CLI versions so developers can report environment differences accurately."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-3-section-6",
        "title": "Linux Permissions, Docker Group, and Rootless Mode",
        "content": "On Linux, access to the Docker daemon is security-sensitive because users who can control a rootful daemon can generally obtain powerful host-level access. The Docker group provides convenient access to the daemon socket, but membership should be treated as a privileged capability rather than an ordinary developer permission. If an organization permits Docker group access, grant it only to approved users and review membership regularly. A user may need to start a new login session before group changes take effect. Avoid solving permission errors by making the Docker socket world-writable, because that exposes control of the daemon to unauthorized local users. Rootless Docker is an alternative in which the daemon and containers run without root privileges, subject to its feature and networking requirements. It can reduce some host-level risks but may require additional setup and may not support every workload identically. Select the mode based on the threat model, required features, operational support, and platform compatibility. Document the chosen access model so troubleshooting does not lead administrators to weaken host security."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-3-section-7",
        "title": "Installation Troubleshooting and Version Management",
        "content": "Docker installation problems should be diagnosed by separating package, service, permissions, network, and compatibility failures. If the CLI is missing, inspect the package installation and executable search path. If the CLI reports that it cannot connect to the daemon, check service state, startup logs, socket configuration, and user permissions. On Linux, systemctl status docker and journalctl -u docker provide useful service evidence. If images cannot be downloaded, test DNS resolution, proxy configuration, firewall rules, registry authentication, and TLS certificate trust. On Desktop platforms, inspect the application status and the health of its virtualization backend. Record docker version, operating-system release, architecture, and installation method before attempting repairs. Avoid deleting Docker data directories as a first troubleshooting step because they may contain important images, volumes, and container metadata. For managed hosts, define an approved Engine version and upgrade window, test upgrades in a representative environment, and maintain a rollback or recovery plan for workloads that depend on runtime behavior."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Dockerfile Instructions",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-1",
        "title": "Dockerfile Purpose, Build Context, and Instruction Flow",
        "content": "A Dockerfile is a text-based build specification that describes how Docker should assemble an image. It contains instructions such as FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, RUN, ENV, EXPOSE, and CMD. During docker build, Docker evaluates the Dockerfile using a build context, which is the set of files made available to the builder. The context is not automatically limited to the Dockerfile directory unless the build command and ignore rules make it so. A command such as docker build -t myapp:1.0 . uses the current directory as the context. This means an unnecessarily large context can slow builds and may expose files that should not be sent to the builder. A Dockerfile should therefore be maintained with a suitable .dockerignore file. Instructions are processed in order, and their results contribute to image layers and build metadata. A clear Dockerfile makes the build reproducible, reviewable, and easier to troubleshoot. Treat it as version-controlled application infrastructure, review changes like source code, and avoid placing passwords or private keys in instructions or build arguments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-2",
        "title": "FROM and Selecting a Reliable Base Image",
        "content": "FROM selects the base image from which a build stage begins. It is normally the first instruction in a stage and establishes the operating-system environment, runtime, and existing filesystem tools available to later instructions. For example, FROM node:22-alpine selects a Node.js image based on Alpine Linux, while a supported Debian-based image may be preferable when native packages require broader compatibility. Base-image selection affects image size, security updates, libc behavior, package availability, and debugging tools. Prefer maintained official or organization-approved images and use an explicit version rather than relying on an ambiguous latest tag. For stronger reproducibility, teams may pin an image digest after validating the update process. A multi-stage Dockerfile can contain multiple FROM instructions, each starting a separate build stage. An optional AS name gives a stage a readable identifier that later COPY --from instructions can reference. Review base images regularly for vulnerabilities and compatibility, and update them through a tested process instead of assuming that a smaller image is always the safest or most suitable choice."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-3",
        "title": "WORKDIR and Predictable Filesystem Locations",
        "content": "WORKDIR sets the working directory for subsequent Dockerfile instructions such as RUN, COPY, ADD, CMD, and ENTRYPOINT when relative paths are used. If the directory does not exist, Docker creates it. Setting an explicit WORKDIR makes the image easier to understand because commands no longer depend on an implicit or inherited current directory. For example, WORKDIR /app followed by COPY package*.json ./ places dependency manifests under /app. A later RUN command then executes from that directory. Prefer absolute paths for WORKDIR so the result is predictable even when the base image defines a different default directory. Avoid scattering commands across unrelated directories because this makes image inspection and maintenance harder. A consistent application layout might use /app for application code, a dedicated non-root user for runtime execution, and a clearly defined writable directory for temporary data. WORKDIR does not itself create application files or install dependencies; it only establishes the directory context for later instructions. Verify file ownership and permissions separately when the container runs as a non-root user."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-4",
        "title": "COPY and ADD for Bringing Files into an Image",
        "content": "COPY transfers files or directories from the build context, or from a named build stage, into the image filesystem. It is generally the preferred instruction for ordinary application files because its behavior is explicit. For example, COPY package.json package-lock.json ./ copies dependency manifests into the current WORKDIR. ADD can perform additional operations such as extracting supported local archive formats and retrieving remote resources in supported builder contexts, so it should be used only when those semantics are deliberately required. Copying the entire project early in a Dockerfile can invalidate dependency layers whenever any source file changes. A more efficient pattern copies dependency manifests first, installs dependencies, and then copies the remaining application source. Use .dockerignore to exclude node_modules, build output, Git metadata, local secrets, temporary files, and other unnecessary content. COPY --chown can set ownership during copying where supported. In multi-stage builds, COPY --from=builder transfers only selected artifacts from an earlier stage, allowing the final image to omit compilers and intermediate files."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-5",
        "title": "RUN and Managing Build-Time Commands",
        "content": "RUN executes commands during image construction and records their filesystem changes in a build layer. It is commonly used to install operating-system packages, download dependencies, compile applications, and create required directories. On Debian-based images, package installation should normally combine package-index refresh and installation in the same RUN instruction, then remove package lists when appropriate. This reduces stale-index problems and avoids retaining unnecessary metadata in a separate layer. Use noninteractive and deterministic package options when builds run in automation. On Alpine-based images, use the package manager appropriate to that distribution rather than copying commands from Debian examples. RUN is different from CMD: RUN executes while building the image, whereas CMD defines the default command for a container at runtime. Avoid putting credentials directly in RUN commands because build history, logs, and image layers may expose them. Use supported BuildKit secret mounts for build-time credentials. Keep build commands understandable and verify their exit codes so a failed dependency installation cannot silently produce an incomplete image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-6",
        "title": "CMD and Defining the Default Container Command",
        "content": "CMD specifies the default command or default arguments used when a container starts. It does not execute during image construction. Docker supports shell-form and exec-form syntax, but exec form is generally preferred for application processes because the executable becomes the container process and receives operating-system signals directly. For example, CMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"] starts Node.js without an extra shell process. A Docker run command that supplies a new command can replace the image CMD. This makes CMD useful as a default that operators can override for debugging or alternative execution. A Dockerfile should normally define one effective CMD; if multiple CMD instructions appear, only the last one is used. CMD should describe the normal runtime behavior, not perform build preparation. For services that need initialization, consider a small entrypoint script with clear signal handling and error propagation. Test graceful shutdown by stopping the container and checking that the application handles termination signals instead of leaving child processes running."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-7",
        "title": "ENTRYPOINT and Combining It with CMD",
        "content": "ENTRYPOINT defines the executable that a container is intended to run. Unlike CMD, it is designed to remain the main command when users supply additional arguments to docker run. Exec-form ENTRYPOINT is usually the safest choice for service containers because the application can receive signals as PID 1. CMD can then provide default arguments for that entrypoint. For example, ENTRYPOINT [\"python\", \"app.py\"] with CMD [\"--port\", \"8080\"] starts the application using the default port, while an operator can replace the arguments at runtime. Shell-form entrypoints introduce a shell process and can complicate signal forwarding unless the script uses exec correctly. An entrypoint script should validate required configuration, avoid printing secrets, and exit with meaningful status codes. Do not use ENTRYPOINT and CMD interchangeably: decide whether users should be able to replace the main command or merely override its arguments. Verify behavior using docker run with no extra arguments and with an explicit argument override."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-8",
        "title": "ENV, ARG, and Configuration Boundaries",
        "content": "ENV defines environment variables that are stored in image configuration and are available to later build instructions and containers created from the image. It is appropriate for non-secret defaults such as an application directory or runtime mode. ARG defines build-time variables that can be supplied during docker build and are generally scoped to the build stage in which they are declared. ARG is useful for controlled build customization, such as selecting a version or build option, but it should not be treated as a secure secret store. Values may appear in build metadata, logs, or cache records depending on how they are used. ENV values are part of the resulting image configuration and can be inspected, so passwords, tokens, and private keys must not be embedded in them. For runtime secrets, use an appropriate secret-management mechanism supplied by the deployment platform. Document which settings are build-time parameters and which are runtime configuration. Test the image with representative environment values and confirm that missing or invalid configuration produces a clear startup error rather than an unpredictable application failure."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-4-section-9",
        "title": "EXPOSE, USER, and Runtime Metadata",
        "content": "EXPOSE documents the network port on which an application is expected to listen inside the container. It does not publish that port to the host. Publishing requires a runtime option such as docker run -p 8080:8080, or a port mapping in an orchestration configuration. EXPOSE is therefore useful metadata for image users but is not a firewall rule or a network listener. USER selects the user and optionally group used for subsequent build instructions and the default runtime process. Running applications as a non-root user reduces the impact of some container compromises and is an important production hardening practice. Create the required user and assign only the filesystem permissions the application needs. Ensure writable directories remain accessible to that user, while application binaries and configuration remain protected from unnecessary modification. Verify the effective identity with docker exec or an application-level diagnostic. Do not assume that EXPOSE makes a service reachable or that USER alone makes an image secure; network publication, capabilities, filesystem permissions, and runtime policy must also be reviewed."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Build Context and .dockerignore",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-5-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding the Docker Build Context",
        "content": "The Docker build context is the collection of files and directories made available to the builder when an image is created. When a developer runs docker build -t web-app:1.0 ., the final dot identifies the current directory as the context. The Dockerfile may be located inside that directory or selected separately with the -f option, but the context determines which local files can be copied into the build. A context can contain application source code, dependency manifests, configuration templates, static assets, and scripts required during image construction. It should not automatically include every file in a developer's working directory. Large contexts increase the time required to transfer data to the builder and can slow remote or BuildKit-based builds. They can also expose local files to a build process that does not need them. Teams should understand the difference between the Dockerfile location and the build-context location, because selecting the wrong context can cause COPY failures or unexpectedly include unrelated files. A deliberate context layout improves build speed, security, and reproducibility."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-5-section-2",
        "title": "Creating and Maintaining a .dockerignore File",
        "content": "A .dockerignore file defines files and directories that should be excluded from the Docker build context. It is placed at the root of the selected context and uses pattern rules to filter content before the builder receives it. Common exclusions include .git, node_modules, local build output, temporary files, test reports, editor metadata, local environment files, and credentials that must never enter an image build. For example, excluding node_modules is useful when dependencies are installed inside the image for the target operating system rather than copied from a developer's machine. The ignore file should be reviewed whenever the application structure changes, because an overly broad pattern can accidentally exclude required source files. Conversely, a missing exclusion can cause slow builds and expose unnecessary data to build steps. Dockerignore patterns are not identical to every shell glob implementation, so validate important rules with an actual build and inspect the resulting context behavior. Treat .dockerignore as a security and performance control, not merely a convenience file."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-5-section-3",
        "title": "Choosing a Minimal and Reproducible Build Context",
        "content": "A minimal build context contains only the files needed to produce the intended image. A common approach is to keep application source, dependency manifests, required scripts, and the Dockerfile in a predictable project structure while excluding generated output and local development data. For a Node.js application, the context might include package.json, package-lock.json, source files, and static assets, while excluding node_modules, coverage reports, and local logs. A reproducible context also avoids depending on files that exist only on one developer's workstation. If a build succeeds locally but fails in CI, compare the actual context contents, ignore rules, file names, and path casing. Linux builders are case-sensitive, so a COPY instruction that works on a case-insensitive workstation may fail in a Linux build environment. Where practical, keep the build context under version control and make CI use the same context path and Dockerfile selection as local development. This reduces hidden dependencies and makes build failures easier to reproduce."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-5-section-4",
        "title": "COPY Path Resolution and Context Boundaries",
        "content": "COPY source destination resolves ordinary source paths relative to the build context, not relative to the Dockerfile's own directory. This distinction is important when a Dockerfile is stored in a subdirectory but the build command uses the repository root as its context. For example, docker build -f docker/Dockerfile . still makes repository-root files available to COPY, subject to ignore rules. A source path that attempts to escape the context with parent-directory traversal cannot be used to access arbitrary host files outside the selected context. This boundary prevents a Dockerfile from freely reading unrelated host directories. Destination paths are resolved inside the image filesystem and should be organized consistently with WORKDIR. When a COPY command reports that a file was not found, verify the selected context, exact relative path, filename capitalization, and .dockerignore patterns before changing the Dockerfile. In CI, also verify that the checkout contains the expected file and that the build command is executed from the intended directory."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-5-section-5",
        "title": "Protecting Secrets and Sensitive Files from Build Context Exposure",
        "content": "Build contexts can contain sensitive files even when the Dockerfile never explicitly copies them into the final image. Build systems may transfer context files to a local or remote builder, so credentials should be excluded before the build begins. Typical sensitive files include .env files, private keys, cloud credentials, local certificate material, password files, and developer-specific configuration. Add appropriate patterns to .dockerignore and use dedicated secret-management features for build steps that genuinely require credentials. BuildKit secret mounts allow a build command to access a secret without intentionally storing it in the resulting image layer, provided the command and Dockerfile are designed correctly. Do not assume that using ARG or ENV makes a secret safe. Also inspect build logs and generated artifacts because commands can accidentally print credentials or copy them into output directories. Establish a policy that secrets are injected through approved CI secret stores and are never committed to source control or packaged as application files."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-5-section-6",
        "title": "Monorepo and Subdirectory Build Context Strategies",
        "content": "Monorepositories often contain several services, shared libraries, deployment manifests, and documentation. Each image should use a context that provides exactly the files required by that service. A context set to the repository root can make shared packages available, but it may also transfer a large amount of unrelated content. A context set to a service directory is smaller, but it cannot directly COPY files located outside that directory. Teams should decide whether shared code should be packaged as a dependency, copied into a controlled build workspace, or built through a repository-root context with carefully designed paths and ignore rules. CI pipelines should make the context explicit rather than relying on whichever directory happens to be the current working directory. For example, a pipeline may use docker build -f services/api/Dockerfile -t api:build . when the repository root is intentionally required. Document this convention so developers do not use inconsistent context paths that produce different image contents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-5-section-7",
        "title": "Diagnosing Build Context and Ignore-Rule Failures",
        "content": "Build-context failures commonly appear as missing-file errors, unexpectedly large build transfers, slow CI builds, or files appearing in an image when they should have been excluded. Begin by recording the exact docker build command, Dockerfile path, and context path. Confirm that the required source file exists relative to the context and is not excluded by .dockerignore. If a file is missing, inspect pattern rules carefully, including directory-level exclusions and any supported negation rules used to re-include selected files. If the context is unexpectedly large, identify large directories such as dependency trees, generated assets, repository metadata, and test output, then exclude only content that is not required. Rebuild after each controlled change and compare build output. Avoid deleting source files simply to make the build succeed. In production CI, record build duration and context-transfer size where available so regressions can be detected early. A small, intentional context is easier to secure, cache, and reproduce."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Image Layers and Caching",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-1",
        "title": "How Docker Image Layers Are Constructed",
        "content": "Docker images are assembled from filesystem layers and image configuration metadata. Many Dockerfile instructions that change the filesystem create a new layer, while metadata instructions may change configuration without adding filesystem content. Each layer represents a set of filesystem changes relative to the preceding image state. When a container starts, the image layers are used as a read-only foundation and a writable container layer is added on top. This design allows multiple containers to share the same underlying image data while keeping their runtime changes separate. A layer is not simply a complete copy of the entire filesystem; modern storage drivers and image formats use content-addressed data and filesystem mechanisms to avoid unnecessary duplication. Understanding layers helps explain image size, build caching, image distribution, and why deleting a file in a later instruction does not necessarily remove its bytes from an earlier layer. Dockerfile design should therefore avoid adding unnecessary files in the first place and should combine related installation and cleanup operations appropriately."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-2",
        "title": "Docker Build Cache and Instruction Reuse",
        "content": "Docker build cache allows a builder to reuse the result of an earlier build step when the relevant instruction and its inputs have not changed. Reusing cached steps avoids repeating expensive operations such as dependency installation and compilation. Cache validity depends on the instruction, its parent state, and the inputs considered by the builder. If a copied source file changes before a dependency-installation instruction, the cache for later steps may be invalidated even when dependency manifests remain unchanged. This is why Dockerfiles commonly copy package manifests first, install dependencies, and then copy the frequently changing application source. Cache reuse is an optimization, not a guarantee that a build is correct. External package repositories and unpinned dependencies can change independently of the Dockerfile, so a cached result may not reflect a newly available package version. Teams should balance speed with reproducibility by controlling dependency versions and deliberately refreshing cache when required."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-3",
        "title": "Dockerfile Ordering for Efficient Cache Utilization",
        "content": "Dockerfile instruction ordering strongly affects how often expensive build steps can be reused. Place stable inputs before frequently changing application files whenever the build process permits it. For a Node.js application, copying package.json and package-lock.json before running npm ci allows the dependency-installation layer to remain reusable when only application source changes. After dependencies are installed, copy the remaining source files and run the build. If all source files are copied before dependency installation, even a small code edit may invalidate the dependency layer and cause a lengthy reinstall. The same principle applies to compiled applications: copy build descriptors and dependency lock files first, resolve dependencies, and then copy source code. Avoid combining unrelated operations into one instruction when doing so makes cache invalidation difficult to reason about. Review the Dockerfile whenever build times increase unexpectedly and use build output to identify which steps are executing instead of being cached."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-4",
        "title": "Cache Invalidation and Changed Build Inputs",
        "content": "Cache invalidation occurs when a builder determines that a previous instruction result cannot safely be reused. Changes to a parent layer, a Dockerfile instruction, copied files, or other relevant build inputs can invalidate the cache for that step and its descendants. For example, changing a dependency lock file should normally cause dependency installation to run again, while changing only a source file copied later should not require reinstalling dependencies. Build arguments and environment settings may also affect cache behavior when they are used by instructions. Remote package repositories introduce another concern: an unchanged RUN command may be cached even though newer packages are available remotely. Use an intentional cache-busting or refresh strategy when an updated package index or dependency is required, rather than adding arbitrary changing values to every build. Understand the difference between a correct cache hit and a stale dependency result. Record which inputs should trigger a rebuild and validate those assumptions with representative source and dependency changes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-5",
        "title": "Inspecting Image Layers and Build History",
        "content": "Docker provides commands that help engineers inspect image metadata and build history. docker history image-name displays the sequence of image history entries, including created-by information and reported layer sizes where available. docker image inspect provides structured information such as image configuration, environment defaults, entrypoint, command, and root filesystem layer identifiers. These commands help explain how an image was assembled, but they do not always reveal every byte-level detail of storage or prove that a sensitive file is absent from every layer. If a secret was copied into an earlier layer and deleted in a later one, the earlier layer may still contain the data. Use a dedicated image-analysis tool when deeper filesystem or vulnerability inspection is required. Compare image history before and after Dockerfile changes to identify unexpectedly large steps. For CI, preserve build logs and image metadata so regressions can be traced to the instruction that introduced them."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-6",
        "title": "Reducing Image Size Without Breaking Runtime Behavior",
        "content": "Reducing image size improves transfer time, storage efficiency, and often deployment speed, but size optimization must not remove runtime dependencies. Start with an appropriate maintained base image and exclude unnecessary files from the build context. Install only the packages needed for the application and remove temporary package metadata in the same build step where practical. Use multi-stage builds to keep compilers, test tools, and development dependencies out of the final runtime image. Avoid copying local dependency directories that contain platform-specific binaries or unrelated files. Inspect image history and filesystem contents to identify large artifacts such as caches, test data, source maps, or duplicate build output. A smaller image is not automatically more secure: a minimal image still needs timely updates, a supported runtime, and appropriate vulnerability review. Test the final image in the same way it will run in production, including health checks, startup configuration, permissions, certificates, and required native libraries."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-7",
        "title": "Build Cache in Continuous Integration Pipelines",
        "content": "CI systems often use temporary build agents, so a local cache may disappear between pipeline executions. BuildKit supports cache import and export strategies that can preserve useful build results across jobs when the CI platform and builder configuration support them. A pipeline can export cache data to an approved registry or cache backend and import it during a later build. Cache scopes should be designed so unrelated branches or projects do not overwrite each other's cache unexpectedly. Consider access control because build caches can contain intermediate filesystem data and metadata. A cache should never be treated as a secure location for secrets. Measure whether cache transfer time is lower than the build time saved; for small images, importing a large cache may cost more than rebuilding. Configure cache behavior explicitly in pipeline scripts and record whether the build used a cache hit, miss, or fallback path. Test cold-cache builds as well as warm-cache builds to ensure the pipeline remains reliable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-6-section-8",
        "title": "Cache Controls, Fresh Builds, and Reproducibility",
        "content": "Sometimes a build must intentionally avoid or refresh cached results. The --no-cache option requests a build without reusing ordinary cached build steps, while --pull asks the builder to check for a newer version of the referenced base image according to the selected tag. These options address different concerns: disabling cache does not automatically guarantee that every external dependency is pinned or that a base image is updated, and pulling a base image does not necessarily force every build instruction to execute. For repeatable production releases, use controlled base-image references, dependency lock files, trusted package sources, and documented build parameters. A fresh build can be useful during incident investigation or after a critical dependency update, but it should be compared with normal cached builds to identify environmental differences. Record the source revision, base-image digest, build arguments, and builder version for release traceability. Reproducibility requires controlling inputs, not merely turning the cache off."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "BuildKit",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-1",
        "title": "BuildKit Architecture and Modern Docker Builds",
        "content": "BuildKit is the modern build engine used by Docker to construct container images. It improves on the traditional builder through a more flexible build graph, parallel execution of independent operations, efficient caching, and support for advanced build features. Instead of treating every Dockerfile instruction only as a simple sequential operation, BuildKit can analyze dependencies between build steps and execute operations when their required inputs are available. It supports features such as cache mounts, secret mounts, SSH forwarding, and improved handling of build contexts. BuildKit is integrated into modern Docker workflows and can also be used through buildx, which provides additional builder management and multi-platform capabilities. Its architecture separates the build client, builder, frontend, and execution workers. This makes it possible to use local or remote builders depending on the development and CI environment. Teams should understand which builder is active because cache behavior, supported syntax, and output options can differ from older build workflows."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-2",
        "title": "Enabling BuildKit and Using Docker Buildx",
        "content": "BuildKit can be used through Docker's modern build command and the Buildx plugin. On supported Docker installations, BuildKit is already the default builder for common workflows. The environment variable DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 can be used in environments where explicit enablement is needed, while docker buildx commands provide builder selection and advanced build options. Start by checking docker buildx version and docker buildx ls to understand which builders are available. A builder may use the local Docker daemon, a container-based driver, or a remote endpoint. Select the builder that matches the required platform and CI architecture. For example, a team may create a dedicated builder for multi-platform builds and use it consistently in release pipelines. Avoid assuming that every workstation and CI agent has identical builder configuration. Record the selected builder and relevant Docker versions when investigating inconsistent builds. Verify the final image by inspecting its metadata and running it on the intended platform."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-3",
        "title": "BuildKit Dockerfile Syntax and Frontend Features",
        "content": "BuildKit supports Dockerfile frontend features that can be selected through a syntax directive at the beginning of a Dockerfile. A directive such as # syntax=docker/dockerfile:1 enables the corresponding Dockerfile frontend version and its supported features. The frontend interprets Dockerfile instructions and converts them into the build operations executed by BuildKit. This allows teams to use capabilities such as cache mounts, secret mounts, SSH mounts, and heredoc syntax where supported. Pinning or controlling the frontend version can improve consistency across developer machines and CI environments. Do not copy advanced syntax into a project without confirming that its configured builder and frontend support it. A Dockerfile may otherwise fail with an unsupported instruction or mount option. Review frontend updates as part of the build-toolchain maintenance process and test them before using new features in production release pipelines."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-4",
        "title": "RUN Cache Mounts for Faster Dependency Installation",
        "content": "BuildKit cache mounts allow a RUN instruction to reuse selected cache data across builds without permanently storing that cache as part of the resulting image filesystem. For example, a package manager cache can be mounted at its expected cache directory while dependencies are installed. A Dockerfile may use RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache to preserve reusable download data between compatible builds. The cache mount is managed by the builder and is separate from the final image layer. This can reduce repeated downloads and improve build speed, especially in CI systems where dependency installation is expensive. Cache contents should be treated as disposable optimization data: a build must still succeed when the cache is empty or unavailable. Choose cache identifiers and sharing behavior carefully when multiple projects or branches use the same builder. Never store credentials in an ordinary cache mount unless the security implications have been explicitly assessed. Validate both warm-cache and cold-cache builds to confirm that correctness does not depend on old cached files."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-5",
        "title": "BuildKit Secret Mounts for Secure Build Operations",
        "content": "BuildKit secret mounts provide temporary access to sensitive values during image construction. They are useful when a build must authenticate to a private package registry or retrieve a protected dependency. A build can receive a secret from a local file or supported environment source and expose it to a specific RUN instruction through a secret mount. The secret is not intentionally committed to the resulting image layer merely because the instruction reads it. This is safer than placing credentials in ARG, ENV, or a copied configuration file. However, the build command itself can still leak a secret if it prints the value, copies it into an output file, or includes it in generated artifacts. Restrict secret access to the exact build step that needs it and use short-lived credentials where possible. In CI, retrieve secrets from the platform's protected secret store and pass them using the supported BuildKit interface. Review build logs and resulting artifacts to confirm that credentials are not exposed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-6",
        "title": "SSH Forwarding and Private Repository Access",
        "content": "BuildKit SSH mounts allow a build step to use an SSH agent or key forwarded from the build client without copying a private key into the image. This is useful when a dependency installation process must access a private Git repository. The Dockerfile declares an SSH mount for the required RUN instruction, while the build command forwards an available SSH agent or approved identity. The private key remains managed by the client-side agent rather than becoming an ordinary file in the resulting image layer. Configure known-host verification so the build validates the identity of the Git server instead of blindly trusting an unknown host. Limit forwarding to the specific instruction that needs repository access. Avoid using a developer's unrestricted personal SSH agent in an automated production build. CI should use a dedicated, narrowly scoped deploy key or short-lived identity. Test the build with the intended repository permissions and ensure that the final image does not contain private keys, agent sockets, or temporary authentication files."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-7",
        "title": "BuildKit Parallelism, Build Graphs, and Progress Output",
        "content": "BuildKit represents build work as operations with dependencies, allowing independent tasks to run concurrently when resources and dependency relationships permit. This can improve build time when a Dockerfile contains separate stages or operations that do not depend on one another. Parallelism is not guaranteed to improve every workload because CPU, memory, disk bandwidth, network throughput, and registry performance may become limiting factors. BuildKit progress output provides information about build steps, cache reuse, and execution status. The plain progress mode is especially useful in CI logs because it produces easier-to-read output than an interactive terminal display. When a build appears slow, identify whether time is spent transferring the context, resolving the base image, downloading dependencies, executing compilation, or exporting the final image. Do not increase builder concurrency blindly on a resource-constrained agent. Measure representative builds and configure resource limits and worker capacity based on actual pipeline demand."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-7-section-8",
        "title": "BuildKit Cache Export, Import, and CI Optimization",
        "content": "BuildKit supports cache export and import so build results can be reused across separate CI jobs or builder instances. A pipeline may export cache metadata and reusable records to a registry or another supported backend, then import that cache during a later build. This is valuable when CI agents are short-lived and local builder storage is discarded after each job. Cache configuration should be consistent across branches and release pipelines, with appropriate separation between projects that must not share intermediate data. Registry access permissions must be limited to approved build identities. Cache export is an optimization and must not become a prerequisite for a correct build. Test the pipeline with no cache available to confirm that a clean build still succeeds. Compare the cost of transferring cache data against the build time saved. Monitor cache hit rates, build duration, storage consumption, and cache failures so the team can adjust the strategy instead of retaining large, ineffective cache artifacts indefinitely."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Multi-stage Builds",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-1",
        "title": "Multi-stage Build Concepts and Build Separation",
        "content": "A multi-stage Docker build uses multiple FROM instructions in one Dockerfile to separate the environment used to build an application from the environment used to run it. The first stage may contain compilers, development libraries, package managers, test tools, and source code. A later stage can start from a smaller runtime image and copy only the application artifacts required for execution. This separation prevents unnecessary build tools from being included in the production image. Each stage has its own filesystem and can be referenced by its stage name or index. Multi-stage builds are particularly useful for compiled applications, frontend asset generation, and services that need different dependencies during development and runtime. They also make the build process easier to express in a single version-controlled file. The final image should contain everything the application needs at runtime, but nothing that is required only during compilation or testing. Always test the final stage independently rather than assuming that a successful build stage guarantees a working runtime image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-2",
        "title": "Naming Build Stages with FROM AS",
        "content": "The AS clause assigns a readable name to a build stage. For example, FROM golang:1.24 AS builder creates a stage named builder. A later instruction can copy selected output from that stage by using COPY --from=builder. Naming stages makes a Dockerfile easier to maintain because references remain understandable when additional stages are inserted or reordered. Numeric stage indexes are possible, but they can become confusing as a Dockerfile grows. Use names that describe each stage's purpose, such as dependencies, test, builder, or runtime-assets. A stage name should be unique within the Dockerfile so references are unambiguous. Teams should review stage dependencies when refactoring the file because a COPY instruction can silently reference a different stage if numeric indexes shift. A clear naming strategy helps developers understand which stage performs dependency resolution, compilation, testing, and final packaging."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-3",
        "title": "COPY --from and Selective Artifact Transfer",
        "content": "COPY --from transfers selected files or directories from another build stage into the current stage. This is the central mechanism that allows a final runtime image to contain compiled application artifacts without inheriting the entire builder filesystem. For example, a Go build stage can compile a binary under /src/output, and the runtime stage can copy only that binary into its required location. The source path is resolved inside the referenced stage, while the destination path is resolved in the current stage. COPY --from can also reference an external image when the build needs a specific artifact from that image. Copy only the files required by the application, such as executable binaries, static assets, certificates, or production runtime metadata. Check ownership, file permissions, and directory structure after copying. A binary compiled against unavailable shared libraries may fail in a smaller runtime image, so validate runtime compatibility rather than assuming that copying the executable is always sufficient."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-4",
        "title": "Building Compiled Applications with Multiple Stages",
        "content": "Compiled applications are strong candidates for multi-stage builds because compilation often requires tools that are unnecessary at runtime. A Java application may use a Maven or Gradle stage to resolve dependencies, compile source, and package a JAR. The final stage can use a suitable Java runtime image and copy only the packaged artifact. A Go application may compile a binary in a Go toolchain image and transfer that binary into a minimal runtime environment. A frontend application may use a Node.js stage to install dependencies and generate static assets, followed by a web-server stage that serves those assets. Each example requires attention to runtime compatibility, native libraries, certificates, timezone data, and file permissions. The build stage should use locked dependencies and reproducible commands. The final stage should be tested using the same startup command and environment assumptions expected in deployment. This reduces image size while preserving the application's actual runtime requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-5",
        "title": "Separate Dependency, Test, Build, and Runtime Stages",
        "content": "A complex Dockerfile can use distinct stages for dependency preparation, testing, compilation, and production runtime packaging. A dependency stage can download or prepare packages using lock files. A test stage can reuse those dependencies and execute unit tests or static checks. A builder stage can produce the final compiled artifact. The runtime stage then copies only approved outputs from the builder. This structure improves separation of concerns and can prevent test frameworks, source files, and compilers from entering production images. However, every additional stage increases Dockerfile complexity and should have a clear purpose. Ensure that the production image is built from the intended final stage rather than accidentally exporting a development or test stage. CI can target named stages when it needs to execute a specific validation step. Record which tests run in the pipeline and ensure that the release artifact is built from the same source revision that passed validation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-6",
        "title": "Targeting Specific Stages with Build Options",
        "content": "Docker build supports targeting a named stage so developers and CI pipelines can build only the required part of a multi-stage Dockerfile. The --target option can select a stage such as test, builder, or runtime. This is useful when a pipeline wants to run tests without exporting the final production image, or when developers need a debugging image containing build tools. A target build may skip stages that are not dependencies of the selected target. Therefore, stage ordering alone does not guarantee that every earlier stage executes. Design the dependency graph so required tests and artifacts are connected to the intended targets. Document the target names used by local development and CI. Before release, build the final runtime target explicitly and validate the resulting image. Avoid publishing a test or debug target under the same production tag because it may contain tools, source code, or configuration that should not be distributed to production environments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-7",
        "title": "External Images and Reusable Build Stages",
        "content": "A multi-stage Dockerfile can copy artifacts from an external image by specifying that image in COPY --from. This can be useful when an organization maintains a trusted artifact image containing certificates, static assets, or approved tooling. It can also allow a build to consume an artifact produced by another pipeline. External image references should be controlled through approved registries and explicit versions or digests. Avoid depending on an untrusted image merely because it contains a convenient file. Verify the origin, integrity, and licensing of externally sourced artifacts. When sharing stages across builds, consider whether a dedicated base image or a separately versioned artifact is easier to maintain than repeating complex build logic. Document the expected directory layout and compatibility contract so downstream Dockerfiles do not break when the source image changes. External image dependencies should be included in release traceability and vulnerability review."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-8-section-8",
        "title": "Debugging Multi-stage Build Failures",
        "content": "Multi-stage build failures commonly involve incorrect stage names, missing artifacts, incompatible runtime libraries, wrong file ownership, or selecting an unintended target. When COPY --from fails, verify that the referenced stage exists and that the source path is present in that stage's filesystem. If the final container starts but the application exits, inspect its logs and verify that the copied binary or package is compatible with the runtime image. Check executable permissions, shared-library dependencies, working directory, environment variables, certificates, and the configured CMD or ENTRYPOINT. Build the relevant intermediate target when you need to inspect an artifact before it is copied. Avoid copying the entire builder filesystem as a shortcut because that removes much of the benefit of multi-stage builds. Use reproducible build commands and preserve build logs so a failed stage can be diagnosed without guessing which intermediate operation produced the problem."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Container Runtime",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-1",
        "title": "Container Runtime Architecture and Lifecycle",
        "content": "A container runtime is responsible for creating and managing the isolated processes that run inside containers. Docker provides a higher-level management experience, while its runtime architecture uses components such as containerd and lower-level OCI-compatible runtimes. The Docker daemon receives API requests, manages images and container metadata, and coordinates container operations. containerd manages container lifecycle tasks, image transfer, and execution coordination. An OCI runtime such as runc performs the lower-level work of creating the container process with the configured namespaces, control groups, mounts, and security settings. A container is not a separate virtual machine with its own independent kernel; it is a group of processes isolated using operating-system features. This distinction affects startup time, resource consumption, security boundaries, and troubleshooting. Understanding the runtime layers helps engineers identify whether a failure belongs to Docker API access, image handling, containerd, OCI runtime execution, or the application process itself."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-2",
        "title": "Container Creation, Start, Stop, and Removal",
        "content": "A container follows a lifecycle that begins when an image is used to create a container instance. The docker create command prepares the container configuration without immediately starting its main process, while docker run combines creation and startup. When the configured process starts, the container enters the running state. If the main process exits, the container normally transitions to an exited state, even when files remain available in its writable layer. docker stop requests a graceful shutdown by sending a termination signal and waits for a configured timeout before forcefully terminating the process if necessary. docker kill sends a signal directly, commonly SIGKILL by default. docker restart stops and starts an existing container, while docker rm removes a stopped container's metadata and writable layer. Persistent application data should be stored in volumes or other external storage rather than relying on the container's writable layer. Operators should inspect the exit code and logs before removing a failed container so useful evidence is not lost."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-3",
        "title": "Namespaces and Process Isolation",
        "content": "Linux namespaces provide important isolation boundaries used by containers. A PID namespace gives a container its own process identifier view, so processes inside it see a different process hierarchy from processes on the host. Network namespaces isolate network interfaces, routes, firewall-related network state, and sockets. Mount namespaces provide a separate view of filesystem mount points, while UTS namespaces isolate hostname and domain-name settings. IPC namespaces isolate interprocess communication resources, and user namespaces can map container user IDs to different host user IDs. These mechanisms help multiple containers operate on one Linux kernel without sharing every process and network resource. Namespace isolation is not the same as a complete security boundary against every kernel vulnerability. Containers still share the host kernel, so kernel patching, capabilities, seccomp, AppArmor or SELinux policies, and workload isolation remain important. When diagnosing an unexpected host or container resource view, identify which namespace the command is executing in before assuming that the host and container should display identical information."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-4",
        "title": "Control Groups and Container Resource Limits",
        "content": "Linux control groups, commonly called cgroups, account for and control resources consumed by groups of processes. Docker uses cgroup functionality to apply resource constraints such as CPU allocation, memory limits, and process counts. A memory limit can prevent one container from consuming all available host memory, although exceeding the limit may cause the kernel to terminate a process through an out-of-memory event. CPU limits influence how much processor time a workload can consume, while CPU shares or weights influence relative scheduling under contention. Resource settings should reflect actual application behavior and expected traffic rather than arbitrary low values. A container that repeatedly restarts under memory pressure may require investigation of heap sizing, memory leaks, traffic spikes, and the configured limit. Inspect container statistics with docker stats and review host-level kernel messages when an OOM event is suspected. Resource constraints are operational safeguards, but they do not replace application-level capacity planning or host monitoring."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-5",
        "title": "Container Signals, PID 1, and Graceful Shutdown",
        "content": "The main process inside a container commonly runs as PID 1 within its process namespace. PID 1 has special signal and child-process responsibilities, so applications that do not handle termination correctly may delay shutdown or leave child processes unmanaged. Docker normally sends SIGTERM during a graceful stop and later sends SIGKILL if the process does not exit within the configured timeout. Applications should handle termination by stopping request acceptance, finishing or cancelling active work safely, closing connections, flushing necessary data, and exiting within the available grace period. Exec-form ENTRYPOINT and CMD instructions help the application receive signals directly instead of placing an unnecessary shell between Docker and the application. If a wrapper script is required, it should use exec to replace itself with the application process. Test shutdown behavior using docker stop and observe both the exit status and application logs. Graceful shutdown is especially important for web services, message consumers, and jobs that process data that must not be interrupted halfway through a critical operation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-6",
        "title": "Container Logging, Exit Codes, and Runtime Inspection",
        "content": "Container runtime troubleshooting depends on collecting evidence before changing the workload. docker logs displays the output captured from the container's standard output and standard error streams, while docker inspect provides detailed configuration and runtime state. docker ps -a shows running and stopped containers, including their exit status. The exit code is useful evidence but should not be interpreted alone: an application may exit with a nonzero code because of invalid configuration, missing dependencies, failed health checks, or an intentional application error. docker events can help reveal lifecycle actions such as start, stop, die, and restart. For deeper inspection, docker exec can run diagnostic commands inside a running container, provided the image contains the required tools and the operator has permission. Minimal images may not include shells or utilities, so use approved ephemeral diagnostic containers or host-level evidence where appropriate. Preserve logs and inspect configuration before deleting or recreating a failed container."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-7",
        "title": "Restart Policies and Container Health Checks",
        "content": "Docker restart policies determine whether a container should be restarted after it exits. Common policies include no, on-failure, always, and unless-stopped. They serve different operational purposes: on-failure is useful when nonzero application exits should trigger retries, while always and unless-stopped support services intended to remain running. A restart policy is not a substitute for fixing an application that repeatedly crashes. Excessive restarts can hide an underlying configuration or dependency failure unless operators monitor restart counts and logs. HEALTHCHECK defines a command that Docker periodically executes to determine whether a container is healthy. A health check can detect an application that is running as a process but not responding correctly. Health status is separate from the container's running or exited state, and Docker alone does not automatically repair every unhealthy application. Choose meaningful check intervals, timeouts, retries, and startup grace periods. Ensure the check measures real application readiness without creating unnecessary load or depending on tools absent from the image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-9-section-8",
        "title": "Container Runtime Troubleshooting and Recovery",
        "content": "Runtime failures should be investigated in layers. First determine whether the container was created, whether its main process started, and whether it exited immediately. Use docker ps -a, docker inspect, and docker logs to capture status, exit code, command, environment, mounts, and error output. If the daemon cannot create the container, inspect daemon health, storage capacity, runtime logs, and host resource pressure. If the container starts and then exits, investigate the application command, missing files, permissions, required environment variables, and external dependencies. If it is repeatedly killed, review memory limits and host OOM evidence. For a shutdown delay, inspect signal handling and child-process behavior. Avoid deleting container storage or restarting the Docker daemon before collecting relevant evidence, particularly on production hosts. Recovery should restore the service using a known-good image and configuration while preserving persistent data and recording the root cause for follow-up remediation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Docker Networking",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-1",
        "title": "Docker Networking Fundamentals",
        "content": "Docker networking allows containers to communicate with one another, reach external services, and expose selected application ports to host systems. Each container receives network configuration that may include interfaces, IP addresses, routes, and DNS settings. Docker provides network drivers that define how containers connect, including bridge, host, none, and overlay networking. The correct driver depends on the deployment environment and required connectivity. A container's internal listening port is not automatically reachable from the host or public network. Operators must understand the difference between a process binding to a container interface, a container joining a Docker network, and a host port being published. Network connectivity also depends on DNS resolution, firewall rules, routing, and the application listening address. A service that works inside a container may still be unreachable from another container or an external client. Troubleshooting should test each layer separately instead of assuming that a successful container start proves that network access is correctly configured."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-2",
        "title": "Bridge Networks and User-defined Bridges",
        "content": "The bridge driver connects containers through a Linux bridge network and is commonly used for single-host Docker deployments. Docker provides a default bridge, but user-defined bridge networks are generally preferable for application stacks. Containers attached to the same user-defined bridge can communicate using container names or network aliases through Docker-managed DNS. This makes service configuration more stable than hardcoding container IP addresses, which can change when containers are recreated. A bridge network also provides network isolation from containers attached to unrelated networks unless explicit connections are configured. Create a network with docker network create and attach containers using the --network option or a Compose network definition. Inspect it with docker network inspect to review attached containers, subnet configuration, gateway, and options. Applications should listen on the appropriate container interface, not only on loopback, if other containers need to connect. Avoid manually assigning overlapping subnets that conflict with host, VPN, or corporate network routes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-3",
        "title": "Port Publishing and Host-to-Container Connectivity",
        "content": "Port publishing creates a mapping between a port on the Docker host and a port inside a container. For example, docker run -p 8080:80 publishes host port 8080 to container port 80. A client connects to the host's published port, and Docker's networking implementation forwards traffic to the container. Publishing a port is different from EXPOSE in a Dockerfile: EXPOSE documents the intended container port but does not create a host mapping. The optional host address controls which host interfaces accept the published connection. Binding to 127.0.0.1 can restrict access to the local host, while publishing on all interfaces may expose the service more broadly, depending on firewall and network configuration. Verify that the application actually listens on the target container port and that no conflicting host process is using the selected host port. Use docker port and docker inspect to confirm mappings. Apply firewall and access-control rules appropriate to the service's exposure requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-4",
        "title": "Container DNS and Service Discovery",
        "content": "Docker-managed DNS provides service discovery for containers connected to user-defined networks. A container can often reach another container by its container name or configured network alias rather than relying on a changing IP address. This is particularly useful for application stacks where a web service connects to a database or cache. DNS resolution works within the relevant Docker network and does not automatically make every container name discoverable from the public network or unrelated networks. When a service name cannot be resolved, verify that both containers are attached to the expected network, inspect network aliases, and examine the container's resolver configuration. A successful DNS lookup does not prove that the destination application is listening or that traffic is allowed. Test name resolution separately from TCP connectivity and application health. Avoid hardcoding container IP addresses in configuration because recreation can assign a different address. Use stable service names and explicit network definitions in Compose or deployment automation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-5",
        "title": "Host, None, and Other Docker Network Drivers",
        "content": "Docker offers several network drivers for different isolation and connectivity requirements. The host driver allows a container to share the host network namespace on supported Linux configurations, reducing network isolation and avoiding ordinary bridge port mapping. It can be useful for specialized workloads but creates port conflicts and changes the container's network boundary. The none driver disables ordinary external network connectivity for the container, leaving only limited local networking. It is useful for isolated tasks that should not communicate over normal container networks. The bridge driver is commonly used for communication on a single Docker host. The overlay driver provides network connectivity across multiple Docker hosts when supported by the deployment platform and configuration. Choose a driver based on actual requirements rather than convenience alone. Host networking should be reviewed carefully because the container shares host networking resources. Validate platform support because networking behavior and available options can differ between Linux Docker Engine and Docker Desktop environments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-6",
        "title": "Overlay Networks and Multi-host Communication",
        "content": "Overlay networking allows containers running on different hosts to communicate through a logical network spanning those hosts. In Docker environments that support overlay networks, the driver uses underlying host connectivity and encapsulation mechanisms to provide container-to-container communication across machines. This is commonly associated with Docker Swarm services and requires appropriate cluster configuration and network reachability between participating nodes. Overlay networking may depend on specific control-plane and data-plane ports being reachable through host firewalls. If those ports are blocked, service discovery or cross-host traffic may fail even when individual containers are healthy. Inspect network membership, node state, service endpoints, and host routing when investigating communication problems. Overlay networking is not automatically available merely because a container is running on a remote machine. For modern orchestrated environments, compare the platform's native networking model with Docker overlay networking before choosing an architecture. Document the network design and validate failover behavior across hosts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-7",
        "title": "Network Isolation, Firewall Rules, and Security",
        "content": "Container network isolation reduces unnecessary communication between workloads, but it must be designed deliberately. Use separate user-defined networks for application tiers that should not have unrestricted access to one another. For example, a public-facing frontend may need access to an internal API, while the database should accept connections only from approved application containers. Published ports should be limited to the interfaces and clients that require access. Host firewall configuration, cloud security groups, routing, and Docker's own network implementation all influence effective reachability. Do not assume that a container is private merely because its image does not contain a public address. Review which ports are published and inspect the host's listening sockets. Avoid exposing database and administrative ports unnecessarily. Apply least privilege to network connectivity and monitor unexpected traffic paths. On production hosts, changes to firewall or Docker network rules should follow change-control procedures because incorrect rules can interrupt unrelated services."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-8",
        "title": "Docker Compose Networks and Application Topology",
        "content": "Docker Compose allows a multi-container application to define its network topology in a version-controlled YAML file. Services attached to the same Compose network can normally discover one another by service name. A frontend service can connect to an API using the API service name and its internal listening port, while the API can communicate with a database over a separate internal network. Compose can create a default project network automatically, and explicit networks can be declared to separate public-facing and internal communication. Service-to-service communication should normally use container ports rather than host-published ports. Publish host ports only when traffic from outside the Compose application needs to enter the stack. Review network membership and aliases when a service cannot resolve another service name. Avoid relying on fixed container IP addresses because Compose may recreate containers with different addresses. Test the complete application topology after changes to service names, network declarations, and port mappings."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-10-section-9",
        "title": "Diagnosing Docker Network Connectivity Failures",
        "content": "Docker network failures should be diagnosed by testing the connection path one layer at a time. Confirm that the source and destination containers are running and attached to the expected network. Use docker network inspect to review membership, subnet, gateway, and network options. Check DNS resolution from the source container, then test whether the destination port accepts TCP connections. Confirm that the application listens on the expected address and port; a service bound only to 127.0.0.1 inside the destination container may not accept connections from other containers. For host-published services, inspect docker port and verify host firewall rules and port conflicts. Check whether VPN routes or overlapping subnets interfere with container traffic. Distinguish DNS failures, routing failures, connection refusals, timeouts, and application-level errors because each points to a different cause. Capture command output and logs before recreating networks or restarting Docker. Make one controlled change at a time and retest the original connection path."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Volumes and Storage",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-1",
        "title": "Docker Storage Model and Writable Container Layers",
        "content": "Docker separates an image's read-only layers from the writable layer created for each container. When a process creates, modifies, or deletes a file inside a running container, the change is normally recorded in that container's writable layer rather than changing the original image. This makes containers easy to replace, but it also means that data stored only in the writable layer is tied to that container's lifecycle. Removing the container removes that writable layer. Persistent application data should therefore be stored in a Docker volume or a deliberately configured bind mount. Understand which files are temporary runtime state and which files must survive container replacement before choosing a storage method. This distinction is essential when designing databases, upload services, logs, and stateful workloads."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-2",
        "title": "Named Volumes and Their Lifecycle",
        "content": "A named volume is a Docker-managed storage resource identified by a stable name, such as app_database_data. It exists independently of an individual container and can be mounted by a replacement container after an upgrade, restart, or recreation. Create one with docker volume create, inspect its configuration with docker volume inspect, and list available volumes with docker volume ls. A container can mount it using the --mount option or the shorter -v syntax. Named volumes are useful for database files, application state, and shared persistent content because Docker manages their storage location. However, a volume is not automatically a backup. Administrators must define ownership, retention, backup frequency, restore testing, and cleanup rules. Avoid removing a volume simply because its original container is no longer running."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-3",
        "title": "Bind Mounts for Host-to-Container Access",
        "content": "A bind mount connects a specific path on the Docker host to a path inside a container. It is useful when developers need to edit source code on the host while a development container reads the same files, or when an application must consume a host-managed configuration file. The host path is explicitly selected, unlike a named volume whose storage location is managed by Docker. Bind mounts can expose sensitive host files or allow a container process to modify important host data, so mount only the required directory and use read-only access where possible. Confirm that the source path exists and that host permissions allow the container process to read or write it. Production deployments should avoid broad mounts such as the entire host filesystem."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-4",
        "title": "Choosing Between Volumes and Bind Mounts",
        "content": "Choose a named volume when Docker-managed persistence is required and the application should not depend on a particular host directory layout. Choose a bind mount when a known host path must be shared directly, such as source code, a controlled configuration file, or a host-provided certificate directory. Both mechanisms can persist data beyond a container's lifetime, but their operational responsibilities differ. Volumes are easier to manage consistently across container recreation, while bind mounts make host paths and permissions part of the deployment contract. Evaluate portability, backup tooling, file ownership, security boundaries, and whether multiple containers need access. Document the selected mount type and destination path so operators can identify where application data actually resides during incidents and migrations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-5",
        "title": "Mount Syntax, Read-Only Access, and Mount Inspection",
        "content": "The --mount syntax makes storage configuration explicit by naming the mount type, source, and destination. For example, --mount type=volume,source=app_data,target=/var/lib/app attaches a named volume at the application's data directory. A bind mount can use type=bind with a host source path. Add readonly when the container only needs to read the mounted content. The -v syntax is shorter, but complex deployments are often easier to review with --mount because its fields are clearly identified. After starting a container, use docker inspect to review its Mounts section and confirm the actual source, destination, and read/write mode. This verification catches incorrect paths, missing mounts, and accidental writes to the container layer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-6",
        "title": "Volume Permissions, Ownership, and Container Users",
        "content": "A mounted directory can exist and still be unusable because the process inside the container lacks the required permissions. Linux evaluates file ownership, group membership, and permission bits using the process identity, which may differ from the host user's identity. Inspect the running process identity and the mounted directory ownership before applying permission changes. Prefer running applications with an appropriate non-root user and granting access only to the required data directory. Avoid solving every permission problem with chmod 777 because it gives broad access and weakens isolation. Some images initialize ownership during startup, while others expect the operator to prepare the volume. Test permission behavior with the actual image and runtime configuration before deploying changes to production."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-7",
        "title": "Sharing Volumes Across Containers",
        "content": "Multiple containers can mount the same named volume when an application architecture requires shared files. For example, a content-processing service may write generated artifacts while a separate web service reads them. Sharing storage does not automatically coordinate concurrent writes, guarantee application-level consistency, or make every filesystem operation safe. Confirm whether the application supports concurrent access and whether file locking or another coordination mechanism is required. Mount read-only in consumers that should not modify the data. Consider failure behavior when one container writes incomplete files or when both services attempt to update the same object. For databases, use the database's supported replication and access model rather than allowing unrelated containers to manipulate its internal data files directly."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-8",
        "title": "Volume Backup, Restore, and Migration",
        "content": "A reliable volume strategy includes tested backup and restore procedures. Identify the volume, determine whether the application must be stopped or placed into a consistent backup mode, and preserve ownership and permissions when exporting its contents. For databases, prefer database-native backup tools or a documented consistency procedure rather than copying actively changing files without coordination. Store backups outside the Docker host so a host failure does not destroy both primary data and its only backup. During restoration, verify the target volume, restore the expected files, apply correct ownership, and test the application before directing production traffic to it. Periodically perform a restore exercise; a backup that has never been restored is not sufficient evidence of recoverability."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-11-section-9",
        "title": "Volume Cleanup, Capacity Monitoring, and Production Operations",
        "content": "Volumes can consume significant disk space even after their original containers have been removed. Use docker system df and host filesystem monitoring to understand storage consumption, and inspect volumes before deleting anything. The docker volume prune command removes unused volumes, so run it only after confirming that no required recovery data or intentionally retained application state will be affected. Establish ownership labels or naming conventions, capacity alerts, retention policies, and documented cleanup approvals. Monitor both free disk space and application-level storage growth because a volume can fill while the host still appears healthy in other areas. Production operations should include capacity forecasting, backup verification, and a clear procedure for expanding or migrating persistent storage without accidental data loss."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Docker Security",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-1",
        "title": "Docker Security Model and Container Isolation",
        "content": "Docker security relies on several layers rather than a single setting. Containers use Linux kernel features such as namespaces to isolate process, network, mount, and other views, while control groups help manage resource consumption. Additional controls include Linux capabilities, security profiles, user identity, filesystem permissions, image provenance, and network restrictions. Containers share the host kernel, so they are not equivalent to separate virtual machines with independent kernels. A vulnerable kernel, an unsafe privileged container, or an exposed Docker daemon can weaken the isolation boundary. Security reviews should therefore examine the host, daemon, images, runtime configuration, secrets, and application dependencies together. Apply defense in depth and avoid assuming that packaging a process in a container automatically makes it secure."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-2",
        "title": "Running Containers as Non-Root Users",
        "content": "A process running as root inside a container can have more authority than an application requires, especially when combined with dangerous capabilities or host mounts. Use the USER instruction in a Dockerfile to select a dedicated non-root account for the application wherever the image supports it. Verify that required directories are writable by that account and that startup scripts do not silently switch back to root. At runtime, the --user option can override the configured identity, but the chosen UID and GID must match the application's file-access requirements. Non-root execution reduces the impact of some application compromises, but it does not replace patching, capability restrictions, or host security. Test the complete startup and shutdown lifecycle under the intended user identity."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-3",
        "title": "Linux Capabilities and Privilege Reduction",
        "content": "Linux capabilities divide traditional root privileges into smaller permissions. Docker removes several capabilities by default, but a container may still receive permissions that its application does not need. Use --cap-drop to remove capabilities and add back only a specifically justified capability with --cap-add. Avoid using --privileged as a shortcut for permission problems because it grants extensive access to host devices and kernel features. Investigate the exact operation that fails, identify the required permission, and test the narrowest configuration that allows it. Review capabilities whenever an image or application changes, since a new entrypoint or dependency may alter its requirements. Least privilege makes container behavior easier to audit and reduces the potential impact of a compromised process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-4",
        "title": "Read-Only Root Filesystems and Temporary Storage",
        "content": "A read-only container root filesystem prevents an application from modifying files in its image-backed filesystem at runtime. Enable it with --read-only when the application supports this operating model, then provide narrowly scoped writable mounts or temporary filesystems for paths that genuinely require writes. Common examples include temporary directories, runtime sockets, and application data directories. Do not make the entire filesystem writable simply because one log or cache path needs modification. Identify every write location through testing and application documentation, and configure tmpfs or persistent storage according to the data's purpose. A read-only root filesystem limits persistence opportunities for an attacker and helps reveal undocumented runtime dependencies before production deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-5",
        "title": "Seccomp, AppArmor, and SELinux Runtime Controls",
        "content": "Docker can apply security profiles that restrict system calls and access to host resources. Seccomp filters limit which system calls a container process may invoke, while AppArmor and SELinux provide additional mandatory access controls where supported and configured on the host. These controls complement namespaces and capabilities by restricting behavior that would otherwise be available to a process. Avoid disabling profiles globally to make an application start. Instead, inspect denial logs, identify the specific operation, and work with the platform security policy to permit only the required behavior. Profile availability and enforcement depend on the host operating system and runtime configuration, so validate the effective settings on the actual deployment platform."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-6",
        "title": "Docker Daemon Socket and API Protection",
        "content": "Access to the Docker daemon is highly privileged because an authorized client can often create containers with extensive host access. Mounting /var/run/docker.sock into an application container can therefore provide a path to control the host's Docker environment. Do not expose the socket to ordinary application containers or untrusted build steps. Restrict membership in the Docker group, protect daemon access, and avoid exposing an unauthenticated remote API on a network interface. Where remote administration is necessary, use a secure transport and carefully controlled identities. CI systems that need to build images should use an architecture designed for the required trust boundary rather than granting every pipeline job unrestricted access to the production daemon."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-7",
        "title": "Image Trust, Minimal Base Images, and Vulnerability Reduction",
        "content": "Container security begins with the image that is deployed. Use trusted base images, prefer maintained releases, and remove unnecessary packages, shells, tools, and build dependencies from the runtime image. Pin image references deliberately and record the source and digest used for a release so the deployed artifact can be identified precisely. Scan images for operating-system and application dependency vulnerabilities, then evaluate severity, exploitability, reachability, and available fixes. A scanner result should lead to an actionable remediation decision rather than being treated as a complete security guarantee. Rebuild images regularly to incorporate base-image updates, and promote the same tested artifact through environments instead of rebuilding different images for each deployment stage."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-8",
        "title": "Secrets Handling and Sensitive Configuration",
        "content": "Passwords, API tokens, private keys, and certificates should not be embedded in Dockerfiles or copied into image layers. Build arguments and environment variables can also expose sensitive values through build metadata, process inspection, logs, or deployment configuration, depending on how they are used. Prefer a dedicated secrets-management mechanism and mount secrets only into the containers that require them. Restrict file permissions, avoid printing secret values during startup, and rotate credentials when exposure is suspected. Separate configuration from image contents so the same immutable image can be deployed safely across environments. Review CI logs, image history, build contexts, and source repositories for accidental secret disclosure, and treat any confirmed leak as a credential-rotation incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-9",
        "title": "Network Exposure, Port Publishing, and Container Boundaries",
        "content": "Publishing a container port makes an application reachable through the host's networking configuration, subject to the host firewall and Docker networking behavior. Publish only the ports required by users or upstream services, and bind to a specific host interface when public exposure is not intended. Containers that do not need external connectivity should use a more restrictive network design. Separate application tiers where practical and avoid placing sensitive administrative endpoints on publicly reachable interfaces. Network segmentation does not replace authentication, authorization, or application-level protection. During a security review, inspect docker ps, docker inspect, published ports, attached networks, and host firewall rules to understand the actual exposure rather than relying only on the Dockerfile's EXPOSE instruction."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-08-topic-12-section-10",
        "title": "Docker Security Auditing, Incident Response, and Hardening",
        "content": "A practical Docker hardening process starts with inventory: identify hosts, daemon versions, running containers, images, mounts, published ports, users, capabilities, and privileged settings. Review Docker daemon configuration and host patch status, scan images, inspect container runtime settings, and verify that sensitive mounts and secrets are tightly controlled. During a suspected compromise, preserve relevant logs and runtime evidence, identify affected images and hosts, and isolate impacted workloads according to the incident plan. Rebuild from trusted sources rather than assuming a compromised container can be cleaned reliably in place. Rotate exposed credentials, patch the underlying cause, and document recovery actions. Repeat the audit after major image, daemon, kernel, or deployment changes so hardening remains part of normal release operations."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Image Tagging and Digests",
    "sections": []
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Registry Integration",
    "sections": []
